4. Dataset and Data Preparation
The notebook loads a file named “delhiaqi.csv”. It renames fields such as date, pm2_5, pm10, no2, and related pollutant columns into clearer names. The Date field is converted into a date format, and additional fields including Year, Month, Month_Name, and Weekday are derived for time-based analysis.
The README identifies the key preparation steps as importing data into a DataFrame, handling missing values, duplicates, and data types, exploring columns and descriptive statistics, creating visualizations, and summarizing the findings.
5. AQI Calculation and Methodology
The notebook calculates an estimated AQI using a weighted combination of pollutant variables. The formula in the submitted code assigns the following weights:
•	PM2.5: 45%
•	PM10: 35%
•	NO2: 10%
•	SO2: 5%
•	O3: 5%
This calculated field is stored as AQI and is then used for the subsequent statistical analysis and visualizations. The report describes it as an estimated AQI because it is the value generated by the notebook's weighted formula.
6. Analysis Performed
•	Overall AQI statistics
The notebook calculates the average, maximum, and minimum estimated AQI and identifies the month associated with the highest and lowest grouped monthly AQI.
•	Monthly AQI analysis
Average estimated AQI is grouped by month and displayed using a combined bar-and-line visualization.
•	AQI distribution
A histogram with a KDE curve shows how estimated AQI values are distributed across the observations.
•	Weekday analysis
A box plot compares AQI distributions across weekdays.
•	Correlation analysis
A heatmap examines correlations between AQI and PM2.5, PM10, NO2, SO2, O3, CO, NO, and NH3.
